Clamp an implausible RTC reading instead of passing it up.

This device's MT6323 RTC is not battery backed. After a full power loss it
comes back reading 2069, hwclock runs in the boot runlevel, and the bad year
becomes the system clock. Every certificate is then expired from the device's
point of view, so anything doing TLS fails with "certificate verify failed" -
an error that points at the CA bundle or the mirror rather than at the clock.
apk was unaffected only because its repositories are plain HTTP.

Stock does not have this problem, and not because the hardware differs. The
vendor driver sanity-checks every read:

    #define RTC_OVER_TIME_RESET  1
    ...
    if (unlikely(time > (unsigned long)LONG_MAX)) {
            rtc_reset_to_deftime(tm);   /* RTC_DEFAULT_YEA 2010 */
    }

  - drivers/misc/mediatek/rtc/mtk_rtc_common.c

That is why a stock Echo Dot shows 2010-01-01 on an RTC that lost its
contents, which is observable: /dev/rtc0 on Device #3 is stamped 2010-01-01.

The vendor test is deliberately NOT copied literally. "time > LONG_MAX" caught
it only because that kernel is 32-bit, where LONG_MAX falls in 2038. On arm64
long is 64-bit and the comparison can never be true, so this uses an explicit
date window instead: [2010, 2060]. The lower bound doubles as the substituted
value so a corrected reading is itself inside the window and cannot re-trigger.

How the bad value gets through is visible in the driver itself:
__mtk_rtc_read_time() does

    tm->tm_year = data[RTC_OFFSET_YEAR];

with no mask, while the alarm path a few functions below does

    tm->tm_year = data[RTC_OFFSET_YEAR] & RTC_AL_YEA_MASK;

RTC_AL_YEA_MASK is 0x7f. The year register is seven bits, so any stray upper
bit lands straight in tm_year.

Read-only, matching the vendor: the register is not rewritten, so a device
whose RTC is merely unset is not made to look like it has been set. The
substitute is in the past rather than the future on purpose - chrony's
makestep steps in either direction, but a clock reading 2069 makes valid
certificates look expired, whereas 2010 makes them look not-yet-valid and
leaves the failure obviously a clock problem.

Measured on this hardware, for the record: the RTC crystal gains about
43 ppm (~3.7 s/day) against the 13 MHz system counter, on stock as well as
here, so the RTC is the less accurate of the two timekeepers and matters only
at boot.
---

diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6397.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6397.c
--- a/drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6397.c
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6397.c
@@ -85,10 +85,37 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The window a reading has to fall inside to be believed.
+ *
+ * RTC_PLAUSIBLE_MIN_YEAR is also the value substituted for a bad one, so that
+ * the substitute is itself inside the window and a second read does not keep
+ * re-triggering. 2010 is the vendor driver's own RTC_DEFAULT_YEA, which is why
+ * a stock device shows 2010-01-01 on an RTC that has lost its contents.
+ */
+#define RTC_PLAUSIBLE_MIN_YEAR	2010
+#define RTC_PLAUSIBLE_MAX_YEAR	2060
+
+static void mtk_rtc_reset_to_default(struct device *dev, struct rtc_time *tm)
+{
+	dev_warn_once(dev,
+		      "RTC read %04d-%02d-%02d is implausible; reporting %d-01-01 instead\n",
+		      tm->tm_year + 1900, tm->tm_mon + 1, tm->tm_mday,
+		      RTC_PLAUSIBLE_MIN_YEAR);
+
+	tm->tm_year = RTC_PLAUSIBLE_MIN_YEAR - 1900;
+	tm->tm_mon = 0;
+	tm->tm_mday = 1;
+	tm->tm_wday = 5;		/* 2010-01-01 was a Friday */
+	tm->tm_hour = 0;
+	tm->tm_min = 0;
+	tm->tm_sec = 0;
+}
+
 static int mtk_rtc_read_time(struct device *dev, struct rtc_time *tm)
 {
 	struct mt6397_rtc *rtc = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
-	int sec, ret;
+	int sec, ret, year;
 
 	do {
 		ret = __mtk_rtc_read_time(rtc, tm, &sec);
@@ -99,6 +126,34 @@
 	/* HW register start mon/wday from one, but tm_mon/tm_wday start from zero. */
 	tm->tm_mon--;
 	tm->tm_wday--;
+
+	/*
+	 * Refuse to pass on a reading the hardware cannot really have held.
+	 *
+	 * __mtk_rtc_read_time() assigns the year register straight into
+	 * tm_year with no mask, unlike the alarm path a few functions below
+	 * which masks it with RTC_AL_YEA_MASK. The register is seven bits, so
+	 * any stray upper bit lands directly in the year: this device comes up
+	 * reading 2069 after losing power, and every certificate on earth is
+	 * then expired from its point of view. That surfaces as
+	 * "certificate verify failed" on anything doing TLS, which points at
+	 * the CA bundle rather than at the clock, and it cost a long
+	 * investigation once already.
+	 *
+	 * The vendor driver does exactly this check
+	 * (RTC_OVER_TIME_RESET in drivers/misc/mediatek/rtc/mtk_rtc_common.c)
+	 * and it is why a stock device reports 2010-01-01 rather than 2069.
+	 *
+	 * NOT a copy of the vendor test, though. Theirs is
+	 * "time > (unsigned long)LONG_MAX", which caught it only because that
+	 * kernel was 32-bit and LONG_MAX fell in 2038. On arm64 long is 64-bit
+	 * and that comparison can never be true, so the bound has to be a real
+	 * date.
+	 */
+	year = tm->tm_year + 1900;
+	if (unlikely(year < RTC_PLAUSIBLE_MIN_YEAR ||
+		     year > RTC_PLAUSIBLE_MAX_YEAR))
+		mtk_rtc_reset_to_default(dev, tm);
 
 exit:
 	return ret;
